  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nCurrent H5 analysis shows west northwesterly flow in place over\nthe region, with a shortwave trough traversing east southeast\nout of the northern High Plains. At the surface, a subtle pseudo\nstationary front is draped west to east near the Interstate 70\ncorridor, with a 1022 mb high centered over Iowa. This has\nresulted in clear skies, light and variable winds, and\ntemperatures in the upper 60s to lower 70s as of 2 PM. Some\npatchy fog may be possible early Wednesday morning, with\novernight lows in the upper 40s to lower 50s.\n\nThe aforementioned mid/upper shortwave trough across the\nnorthern High Plains will quickly move eastward into the Upper\nMississippi Valley by early Wednesday morning and dig further\nsouth into the Central CONUS by Wednesday afternoon, with the\naccompanying surface low moving eastward across Iowa and into\nnorth central Illinois. This will send a cold front through the\nCWA tomorrow morning, with winds turning westerly and eventually\nnorthwesterly. Winds will increase by late Wednesday morning,\nwith gusts as high as 25 to 30 mph through mid afternoon. Cold\nair advection behind the front will yield cooler temperatures \nfor tomorrow, with afternoon highs forecast to reach the upper \n50s to mid 60s. Winds should begin to relax by tomorrow evening\nas the pressure gradient slackens, and light winds and mostly\nclear skies Wednesday night into Thursday morning should allow\nfor temperatures to drop into the upper 30s to lower 40s.\n\nA reinforcing shot of even colder and drier air should move\nnorth to south through the region Thursday morning, yielding\nhighs in the upper 40s to lower 50s on Thursday afternoon. With\nlight winds and mostly clear skies again Thursday night, the\nentire CWA is forecast to dip below freezing early Friday\nmorning. Due to the growing season being over, we will not be\nissuing any frost/freeze products for portions of the CWA that\nhave not received a freeze yet. Surface high pressure builds in\ndirectly overhead on Friday, with the region still under the \ninfluence of the deep closed mid/upper low over the Great Lakes \nand northerly flow aloft. Highs for Friday should only reach the\nmid 40s to lower 50s, with another freeze expected CWA wide \nFriday night into Saturday morning. \n\nAt least marginally warmer temperatures are probable this \nweekend into early next week, but just how much warmer is \nunknown at this time. NBM MaxT 25th and 75th percentiles for MCI\non Sunday is 48 degrees and 59 degrees, respectively, with 49 \nand 61 degrees, respectively, for Monday. Some guidance wants to\nbring a shortwave trough into the Southern Plains on Sunday,\nwhich will bring in some chance PoPs (20% to 30%).",
